{
  "good": "As I understand it, depth allows the network to build complex functions through compositionality, creating a richer space of possible mappings. However, making a network too deep can introduce severe training difficulties. On the other hand, width is great for computational efficiency because it allows for parallelization on hardware. Furthermore, wider networks often produce feature representations that are easier for us to interpret.",
  "partial": "Width is clearly beneficial because it allows for parallel processing, which is great for modern hardware. Also, wider networks tend to have more interpretable features. Depth is important because it helps the network map complex functions, but the lecture mentioned that if you just make it really deep, the training process can become unstable and difficult to optimize.",
  "wrong": "I think the main difference is that wide networks are just faster because they have more neurons running simultaneously, which is good for speed. Deep networks are better because they can handle more complex functions, which is the whole point of deep learning. The choice really just comes down to whether you need a fast answer or a highly accurate one, and depth always wins on accuracy."
}

{
  "good": "For highly oscillatory functions, the deep network is vastly more efficient because depth allows the network to recursively build up complexity. Just as the triangle function doubles its kinks with each layer, a deep architecture can generate many local extrema by composing simple functions. A shallow network would require an impractically massive width to encode the same level of high-frequency detail, demonstrating the exponential advantage of depth over width.",
  "partial": "I think depth is better because it allows the network to build up complex features layer by layer, which is necessary for oscillations. The lecture showed that increasing depth leads to an exponential increase in the number of kinks. However, a shallow network would still be able to approximate the function, it would just need to be very wide, which is inefficient.",
  "wrong": "I would argue that width is actually better for highly oscillatory functions because it gives the network more parameters to fit all the tiny changes. While depth is good for general complexity, a very wide network can capture high-frequency signals more directly. The exponential growth in depth is probably just a theoretical bound and might not be practical in real-world training."
}

{
  "good": "Basically, the big theoretical change is that instead of thinking huge models always overfit, the new idea is that deep networks actually learn simple functions that generalize well. We train them by using a loss function, like cross-entropy, which tells us how wrong the model is. We then repeatedly adjust all the weights—like 'wiggling around'—to minimize that error across the training data. Mathematically, all these models are built using tensors, which are multi-dimensional arrays that allow us to run massive calculations in parallel on GPUs.",
  "partial": "The main idea is that deep learning models are structured using tensors, which are just multi-dimensional arrays that let us use GPUs for fast parallel processing. We train them by calculating a loss, such as cross-entropy, which measures the difference between the model's prediction and the correct answer. The goal is to adjust the weights until the loss is small, but I don't really get the whole shift from the old overfitting theory to the new one.",
  "wrong": "The core concept is that deep learning is all about making the model as complex as possible so it can fit every single data point perfectly. We use loss functions to figure out which weights need to be changed, and we keep adjusting them until the error is zero. The whole thing is just about using massive matrix multiplications, which is why GPUs are so important for speeding up the process."
}

{
  "good": "I think the main difference is that simple MLPs are just chains, but a general DAG can have components that split or combine. To handle this, we introduce branching and merging operations into the backpropagation process. When gradients reach a merge point, they need to be aggregated, which is why they are summed up. This allows the backprop algorithm to correctly flow through any complex, non-linear graph structure.",
  "partial": "The extension requires us to account for structures that aren't just linear chains. We need to define operations for when paths split (branching) or when they come back together (merging). The key is that these operations allow the gradient to pass through the entire DAG. However, I'm not totally sure about the specific rule for how the gradients are combined at the merge points.",
  "wrong": "The difference is mostly about how the computational graph is defined. For a DAG, you just have to ensure that the nodes are ordered correctly so that the forward pass works. Backpropagation itself doesn't change much; you just need to make sure that the gradient calculation is applied to every single node in the graph, regardless of whether it's a chain or a complex structure."
}

{
  "good": "I think the final step requires us to apply the learning rate, $\\eta$, and transpose the resulting matrix. The partial derivative calculation gives us the direction of change, but we need the learning rate to control the size of that step. Transposing is necessary because the dimensions of the input and output gradients need to align correctly when we add the update back into the original weight matrix.",
  "partial": "The update is achieved by multiplying the input to the function by the output gradient, which gives us the necessary gradient for the weights. This multiplication is a huge simplification because of the linear nature of the layer. We then add this resulting matrix to the original weights to move them in the direction that minimizes the cost.",
  "wrong": "We must first calculate the input gradient, and then we multiply that by the output gradient. The final step is to simply scale this product by the learning rate. This scaling tells us how aggressively we want to change the weights based on the error we calculated during the backward pass."
}

{
  "good": "The number of parameters for a single filter is found by multiplying the spatial size (5x5 = 25) by the number of input channels (64), resulting in 1600 parameters. The total number of filters in the bank must match the desired number of output feature maps, which is 128. Each of these 128 filters then performs a weighted linear sum across all 64 spectral bands to produce one single output dimension.",
  "partial": "To find the parameters for one filter, you multiply the spatial dimensions (5x5) by the input channels, so each filter needs 1600 parameters. Since we want 128 output feature maps, we will need 128 total filters in the bank. This process allows the model to learn 128 different representations from the input data.",
  "wrong": "The total number of filters would be 64, because that is the number of input channels we are starting with. For a single filter, the parameters are determined by the spatial extent, which is 25, since the input channels are handled separately. We would then use the 128 output channels to define the final size of the feature map."
}

{
  "good": "Neural networks are modeled as Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs), which allows us to define complex data flow beyond simple chains. When gradients flow back through a DAG, specific operations apply: for instance, if information branches, the gradients from both paths must be summed. This structure also supports parameter sharing, allowing the same weights to be reused across different parts of the network for efficiency. Ultimately, the entire process of defining and executing these forward and backward passes is what is defined as differential programming.",
  "partial": "The structure of a neural network is based on a DAG, which allows for complex connections like splitting or merging information. This structure is key because it enables parameter sharing, meaning we can reuse the same weights in multiple components. The entire training process, which involves defining these forward and backward passes, is essentially an application of differential programming.",
  "wrong": "A neural network uses a DAG to manage how data moves from input to output. Parameter sharing is simply a way to duplicate weights so that different parts of the network can access the same information. Differential programming is just the mathematical tool we use to calculate the loss function, and the DAG just helps us visualize the data flow during the forward pass."
}

{
  "good": "The field started with a lot of hype after the 1958 perceptron, but that initial excitement quickly faded after Minsky and Papert showed its mathematical limitations in 1972. Things picked up again in the 1980s when backpropagation allowed for multilayer networks to solve complex problems like XOR. However, a second 'AI winter' hit around 2000 because, despite the theory, we lacked the efficient hardware and programming methods. The field finally exploded in 2012 when AlexNet used GPUs and the massive ImageNet dataset to achieve a major breakthrough.",
  "partial": "Neural networks first gained traction with the perceptron in the 1950s, but early critics pointed out its limitations, causing a major setback. The field saw a big comeback in the 1980s thanks to backpropagation, which enabled us to build deeper, more powerful networks. Although there was a period of skepticism around 2000, the real turning point came in 2012. AlexNet proved that deep learning could work, primarily by using powerful new hardware.",
  "wrong": "The history of deep learning is really about the constant struggle between theory and application. Initially, the perceptron was seen as a perfect model of the brain, but the limitations were too severe. The biggest breakthrough was actually the development of convolutional neural networks in the 1990s. The reason these networks finally succeeded was because researchers figured out how to write better algorithms, not because of new hardware or massive datasets."
}

{
  "good": "The total error is found by summing the maximum possible error from each of the $N$ strips. Since the area of one triangle is $1/2 L/N^2$, multiplying this by $N$ gives a total error bound of $1/2 L/N$. To ensure the approximation is accurate enough, we require this total error to be less than or equal to our target $\\epsilon$. By setting $1/2 L/N \\le \\epsilon$ and rearranging the terms, we find that the number of strips $N$ must be greater than or equal to $1/2 L/\\epsilon$.",
  "partial": "The total error is found by multiplying the area of one rectangle by the number of strips, $N$. This calculation simplifies the total error bound to $1/2 L/N$. We then set this bound to be less than or equal to the desired error $\\epsilon$. From this inequality, we can see that $N$ needs to be proportional to $L/\\epsilon$, but I'm not sure if I need to include the factor of $1/2$ when solving for $N$.",
  "wrong": "The total error is found by multiplying the area of one rectangle by $N^2$, because the error scales quadratically with the number of strips. This gives a total error of $1/2 L$. We want this total error to be less than $\\epsilon$, so we set $1/2 L \\le \\epsilon$. Solving for $N$ is unnecessary here because the total error doesn't depend on $N$ in this model, which seems wrong."
}

{
  "good": "The activation function is critical because without non-linearity, a neural network would only be able to model simple linear relationships, which is insufficient for complex data. Functions like Tanh are bounded, but they suffer from gradient saturation, meaning the gradients shrink to zero for very large or small inputs, which stalls learning. ReLU is often preferred because it is computationally efficient and helps speed up convergence. However, we must be careful, as ReLU can lead to 'dead units' if the input is strongly negative, causing gradient collapse and preventing that part of the network from learning.",
  "partial": "Activation functions are necessary because they introduce the non-linearity required for a neural network to solve complex problems beyond simple straight lines. Tanh is a common early choice, but its bounded nature can cause issues with the gradient flow. ReLU is widely used in practice because it is very efficient and helps the model train faster. However, the choice is not perfect, as ReLU can sometimes cause problems if the inputs are too negative.",
  "wrong": "The activation function is important because it determines the final output shape of the model, which helps make the predictions look more realistic. If the function is too complex, the model might be too slow to run on the GPU. Functions like Tanh and ReLU are chosen based on how well they handle the input data's distribution, ensuring the network can process the information quickly and efficiently. The main goal is usually just to make sure the output values are within a reasonable range."
}

{
  "good": "A local minimum is a point where the loss function is low, but it's not the absolute lowest point (the global minimum). If a landscape has multiple local minima, the starting point of the network's weights determines which minimum the optimization process settles into. If we start in a basin of attraction for a local minimum, we might never find the true global minimum. However, some landscapes, like the water flow analogy, are designed so that all starting points lead to the single best minimum.",
  "partial": "Local minima are just points where the gradient is zero, and the global minimum is the lowest possible loss. The starting point is really important because if you start in a bad spot, the network might get stuck in a local minimum instead of finding the best one. But sometimes, the landscape is so smooth that no matter where you start, the optimization process will naturally flow toward the best minimum.",
  "wrong": "The difference is that local minima are flat spots, and global minima are steep spots. The starting point doesn't really matter because the gradient will always push the network toward the lowest point, which is the global minimum. If there are multiple minima, they are all equally good, so the network just picks one randomly."
}

{
  "good": "Deep networks are built as a sequence of simple computations, where each layer adds a non-linearity. This structure allows the model to build increasingly abstract representations as the data passes through the layers, giving it a much higher capacity. In contrast, a shallow network is limited; unless it is extremely wide, it can only efficiently handle basic tasks like identifying simple edges. Therefore, deep networks are necessary to tackle complex problems, such as translating languages or recognizing specific objects.",
  "partial": "The main difference is that deep networks have more layers, which gives them a higher capacity than shallow networks. This increased capacity allows deep nets to learn more complex functions and representations. Shallow networks, however, are simpler and are generally only good for basic computations. The depth itself is what allows the model to handle harder problems.",
  "wrong": "Shallow networks are actually more efficient because they have fewer parameters, which makes them faster to train. Deep networks, while they can handle complex tasks, are often overkill and tend to overfit the training data because they have too many layers. The complexity of a problem is more related to the size of the dataset than the depth of the network structure itself."
}

{
  "good": "The most likely cause is a data format mismatch between what the model expects and what we are providing, such as using BGR when the model was trained on RGB, or loading images upside down. This mismatch causes the model to misinterpret features, like confusing a red channel for blue. To diagnose this, we must inspect the data pipeline thoroughly before training or inference. Specifically, we need to check the tensor shape, data type (e.g., uint8), and ensure that the min, max, and mean values are consistent across both training and testing data.",
  "partial": "The failure is probably due to a mismatch in the data pipeline, meaning the data we feed the model doesn't match the format it was trained on. For example, the library might be permuting the color channels, like using BGR instead of RGB. To fix this, we need to ensure consistency between training and inference. I would start by checking the shape and data type of the tensors to see if they are what the model expects.",
  "wrong": "The poor performance is probably because the model architecture itself is too shallow to capture the complexity of the objects, or perhaps the learning rate is set too high. If the model is struggling, we should try adjusting the optimizer parameters or adding more layers to the network. Data issues are usually minor, so I would first focus on optimizing the training loop before worrying about preprocessing steps."
}

{
  "good": "The model failed because the spaceship image is fundamentally out of distribution, meaning it does not belong to the population the model was trained to handle. While the model showed impressive generalization to cat sketches, those inputs were still semantically related to the target concept. Since the neural net is simply a mapping from X to Y, it cannot produce a meaningful output when the input is completely unrelated to its learned domain. To prevent this failure, the input must be constrained to the domain of the target concept, ensuring it is systematically related to the data it was trained on.",
  "partial": "The model failed because the spaceship is too far outside the distribution of the training data. The model learned a specific mapping from edge maps to cats, and while it could generalize to related inputs like hand-drawn sketches, the spaceship is a completely different concept. To fix this, we should only feed the model inputs that are clearly related to cats or edges, keeping them within the population it has learned to generalize from.",
  "wrong": "The model likely failed because the spaceship image contains too many complex features that the network couldn't process effectively. The model might have hit a bottleneck in its internal layers when trying to map such a highly abstract input. To prevent this, we should probably increase the depth of the neural network or use a different activation function to give it more capacity to handle complex, novel inputs."
}

{
  "good": "The lecture explains that we can approximate a hyperrectangle by using a clever trick involving axis-aligned hyperrectangles. First, we sum up hyperrectangles that are aligned along each dimension, which creates a plus-shaped surface where the center intersection is twice as high. To isolate only the intersection point, we subtract $d-1$ from this sum and then apply a ReLU function. This final step effectively slices off the surface, leaving only the desired hypercube.",
  "partial": "The method starts by taking several hyperrectangles that are aligned with different axes and adding them together. This creates a complex surface shape. The goal is to use ReLU functions to filter this shape so that only the central intersection region remains. This allows the network to approximate the desired hyperrectangle in higher dimensions.",
  "wrong": "The segment suggests that we can approximate a hyperrectangle by simply stacking multiple two-layer ReLU networks on top of each other. We use the ReLU functions to scale the size of each individual hyperrectangle. By adjusting the weights, we can make the overall network output look like a perfect hypercube, which is then scaled up to approximate the target shape."
}

{
  "good": "The full Hessian naturally splits into two components: one related to the error function and one related to the model's curvature. The Gauss-Newton method simplifies this by assuming the error measure is the squared error. This specific assumption allows us to effectively ignore the second-order information about the model itself, which is the curvature term. By dropping this term, the Hessian is approximated simply as the product of the model's derivatives, making the optimization tractable.",
  "partial": "The Hessian of the composite function breaks down into two pieces, representing the curvature of the error and the curvature of the model. To simplify the calculation, the Gauss-Newton method makes an assumption about the error function. This allows us to drop one of the terms from the decomposition. This simplification results in the Hessian being approximated by the product of the model's derivatives, which is necessary for the iterative update step.",
  "wrong": "The full Hessian decomposes into two parts, which is essentially a way of applying the chain rule to the second derivative. The Gauss-Newton method simplifies this by assuming that the objective function is locally linear. This assumption allows us to ignore the complex curvature terms entirely. By doing this, we can replace the full Hessian with a simpler matrix formed by the product of the model's derivatives, which speeds up the optimization process."
}
